module gunbc.rung_drop.long_module_changed_witness_cpu_observed_only

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.rung_drop { RungDrop, Standing, TypedDeclaration, ReplacementStaged }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, MechanicallyPreventable }

// ============================================================================================
// THE DECLARED RUNG DROP for the CPU line over a changed witness in a long module (DESIGN 4b(3)).
// ============================================================================================
//
// WHAT THE ARM DOES AND WHY IT IS A DROP AND NOT A FIX. A changed witness in a test/claim/long
// module now runs under CpuDeadlineObservedOnly: its CPU is measured and published and decides
// nothing, while the verdict still gates and the wall safety deadline stays armed. That is the
// same treatment a cost-debt row already receives, and the operator ruled it the right one -- but
// it REMOVES a wall that was standing, so it is declared here rather than landed silently. What is
// lost is exactly one thing: the CPU ceiling's power to turn a reached verdict into a floor red,
// for witnesses in the long home.
//
// WHY THE WALL WAS NOT DOING THE WORK IT LOOKED LIKE IT WAS DOING. These witnesses are never
// planned on an ordinary floor -- the long home exists because their rows exceed the per-PR budget,
// and that cadence has been declared-unrealized since falsifier.yml was deleted. No green run has
// ever paid their cost. The line applied only when a PR TOUCHED their file, which made them changed
// witnesses. So the wall did not protect the floor from an expensive witness; it converted "this PR
// touched a long module" into three reds that say nothing about the change. The instrument is the
// floor's own per-claim cost receipt for gunbc#11004 at 6b153bf8 -- the run's BUDGET-REFUSED
// announcements and the required-floor-claim-cost artifact beside them -- which reports every one of
// the three refused at the line before reaching a verdict, and no row for the module in any green
// floor's artifact at all.
//
// WHAT THE DROP IS NOT. It is not a raised ceiling and not a new lane: both are refused for this
// class at docs/plans/floor-cost-distribution-near-the-ceiling.md, which says reducing the shared
// fixed cost is the only lever. It is not a widening either -- a changed witness outside the long
// home and off the cost-debt roster still reds at the line, and
// v2.test.floor_changed_witness.changed_short_module_witness_still_takes_the_ordinary_policy is the
// control that keeps that true.
//
// THE RESTORATION TRIGGER NAMES A CAPABILITY, NOT AN ARTIFACT, and the capability is the one that
// makes the line honest rather than the one that makes these three rows pass. The cost is a FIXED
// per-frame front end -- language model plus tokenize, parse and normalize, rebuilt in every claim's
// own evaluation frame -- and "fixed" is what the cost receipt reports rather than an inference from
// it: across the three witnesses the receipt orders them INDEPENDENTLY of their source sizes, with
// the shortest source the slowest, and a paired before/after across a source-shortening change moved
// two of the three the WRONG WAY. Shrinking sources cannot reach it because there is no size term to
// shrink. Netting it across claims is what would, and the one attempt that netted it (a WARM shared
// producer) then failed at consumption, which is the serve-boundary defect this trigger also names.
data long_module_changed_witness_cpu_observed_only: RungDrop = RungDrop {
  identity: "long_module_changed_witness_cpu_observed_only" as NonEmptyStr,

  subject: "the 500ms per-claim CPU ceiling over a CHANGED witness in a test/claim/long module: a wall removed for a population whose cost is a shared fixed front end rather than the witness's own work",

  declared: "2026-09-11",

  standing: Standing,

  declaration: TypedDeclaration {
    previous: MechanicallyPreventable,
    temporary: Mitigatable,
    reason: ReplacementStaged { replacement: "shared-producer netting of the front end that survives consumption" },
    population: [
    "v2.workflow.required_floor changed_witness_expensiveness_is_declared (the long-home arm: a changed witness whose module matches long_home_prefixes runs for its verdict, exactly as a cost-debt row does)",
    "v2.workflow.required_floor changed_witness_cost_policy (the join that selects it, and the only place the two conditions meet)",
    "src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run/required_floor_runner.rs (the seed realization of the same arm, keyed on the long_home prefix match the runner already computes)",
    ],
    restoration_trigger: "the per-frame front end is paid once per run rather than once per claim, so a claim's measured CPU is its own work: a shared producer whose value SURVIVES CONSUMPTION in the claim frame that reads it. Both halves are required: netting a producer whose value the consuming claim cannot use would move the cost without removing it, so survival of consumption must be DEMONSTRATED at retirement rather than assumed. The netting half is already demonstrated, and the producer of that fact is the floor's own pure-producer-share-warm phase receipt on the run that enrolled them: three normalized-tree producers reported disposition=Stored at strict preparation, with their fill billed to preparation rather than to any claim. The consumption half is NOT known to fail, and this row previously said it did: it cited every claim reading a served normalized tree erroring with no field 'kind' on type 'NormalizedTree'. That error was a type error in the probe fixture on the measuring branch, which passed a NormalizedTree where ProbeNormalized.root is declared Node; gunbc#11025 repaired it on main and the measuring head did not carry that repair, so both readings of it were of the same authored defect and neither was evidence about the serve boundary. The withdrawal is recorded here rather than dropped silently because the claim is what scoped this row. What remains, and what keeps the row standing, is the measured COST, which none of that touches: the front end is a FIXED per-claim charge with no size term -- it is paid once per claim whatever the claim is -- and the floor's own per-claim cost receipt reports all three claims refused at the line before reaching a verdict. A fixed charge with no size term is precisely a cost the witness does not cause, so until netting is on, the 500ms line is measuring the front end and not the population it is meant to bound. Retirement is observed and not asserted: when the front end is closure-scoped and the three v2.test.long.accumulator_copy_compile_gate witnesses complete under the armed line, this arm and this row retire together. A run in which they merely pass under the OBSERVED-ONLY policy does not discharge it, because that is the state this row declares",
  }
}
